\section{Descriptions}{

When the researcher described the files with \code{\link[=describe_output]{describe_output()}},
\code{inspect()}
\itemize{
\item checks a table by the count and grouping columns named there instead
of guessing them, and sends it back when a named column is missing,
\item checks the numbers behind a figure, and sends the figure back when
they need changes or are missing (the figure itself is still reviewed
by hand),
\item checks the description file like other text, because the researcher
wrote it,
\item shows the descriptions in \code{\link[=write_report]{write_report()}}.
}

With \code{require_description = TRUE} in \code{\link[=mimic_config]{mimic_config()}}, every output
file without a description is sent back.

A table of model coefficients is approved when it carries
\code{df_residual} (and \code{observations}) at or above the limits, as tables from
\code{\link[=safe_model]{safe_model()}} do; otherwise it is reviewed by hand.
}

\section{With the real data}{

Some rules can only be checked against the microdata. Give \code{real_data}
and \code{inspect()} also
\itemize{
\item applies the dominance rules with \code{\link[=check_dominance]{check_dominance()}} to tables of sums
and means: a column such as \code{mean_income} or \code{sum_income} is matched to
the variable \code{income}, and the table's grouping columns to the
variables of the same name,
\item flags numbers in tables that equal a value held by fewer than
\code{min_count} persons (often a median or extreme of a small group),
\item searches text files, the text cells of tables and file names for
identifiers, free text and rare values (a day-precise date, a code or a
settlement held by fewer than \code{min_count} persons).
}

Persons are found through identifier columns in the real data: names
like \code{person_id}, \code{cpr...} or \code{pnr...}, and the \code{id_vars} of the
configuration. Add a key with another name (\code{lbnr}) to \code{id_vars}, or
rare values are counted in rows.
}

\section{Counts are taken as persons}{

A count column is read as a number of persons, because that is what
\code{min_count} is about. A table of contacts per settlement can pass with 12
contacts that all belong to one person. Without the real data \code{inspect()}
cannot tell; with \code{real_data} a label held by fewer than \code{min_count}
persons is found whatever the count next to it says. Ask for counts of
persons, not of rows, when the script works on a linked table.
}

\section{Earlier releases}{

The most realistic differencing attack does not use two tables from one
run but two runs: the same table for all persons, and a month later for
all persons except one settlement. Each run has its own empty output
directory, so \code{inspect()} only sees it with a register. Record what is
released with \code{\link[=record_release]{record_release()}} and give the same \code{register} every
time: each table is then also compared with every table released before.
}

